  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 AM CDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nAfter this area of heavy rain exits the southeast this morning, a \nmuch less active pattern will settle in for the week. Behind the \ncold front moving through IL currently, a high pressure center will \nset up over the Upper Mississippi Valley / Great Lakes region for a \nday. Temperatures for the next 7 days will be in the upper 70s to \n80s for highs and 60s to low 70s for lows. This period of cooler \nweather doesn't look like it is in for the long haul. The CPC 6-10 \nand 8-14 day outlooks show a 30-50% chance of above normal \ntemperatures. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms re-enter the forecast by late \ntomorrow as a upper disturbance pulses through. SPC has central \nIL in a marginal risk for severe weather for Tuesday west of a \nPittsfield to Ottawa line, which would be for the late afternoon\nconvection. There is a 50-70% chance for showers and \nthunderstorms tomorrow afternoon into Wednesday morning. \n\nAdditional chances for rain exist through Thursday before returning \nto a mostly dry forecast for the end of the week. Wednesday and \nThursday are not expected to have any severe weather at this time. \nIt also appears that the majority of the rain that may occur those \ntwo days should be south of I-74/Danville, providing a well deserved \nbreak from the heavy rain.",
